input.script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'transport-game-review',
  description: 'Multi-dimension review of fun/transport (code + live play) with adversarial verification and an implementation plan',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'specialist reviewers read code and play the game' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'skeptics check each proposal against code, docs and live game' },
    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'dedupe, rank and group into an implementation plan' },
  ],
}

const SP = 'REDACTED'
const CONTEXT = `
PROJECT: the browser game "Transport" at /Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/transport — an isometric transport & city-building game (static ES modules, no build step, Canvas 2D). Read fun/transport/README.md (player guide), ARCHITECTURE.md (module reference) and PERFORMANCE.md first — they are long and describe many existing features; do NOT propose things that already exist.
Key files: app.js (UI/input, ~97 KB, long lines), model.js (simulation/state), renderer.js, construction-plan.js, route-planner.js, gameplay-insights.js, settlements.js, industry-simulation.js, environment.js, economy-pricing.js, data.js, buildings.js, chains*.js, saves*.js, visibility*.js, compact-play.js/.css, style.css + other css.
Tests: node --test --test-concurrency=4 fun/transport/tests/*.test.mjs (run from repo root; 417 pass today, ~55 s). Browser checks live in fun/transport/tests/*-browser-check.mjs.

LIVE GAME: a no-cache static server is already running: http://127.0.0.1:8841/fun/transport/ . Drive it with standalone Playwright scripts written to YOUR scratch dir (${SP}/review/<your-dimension>/) and run with node. Import Playwright from '/Users/vojta/Dev/evangelist-exchange/node_modules/playwright/index.mjs' and launch chromium.launch({channel:'chrome', headless:true, args:['--use-angle=metal','--enable-gpu','--ignore-gpu-blocklist']}). Chrome CANNOT launch inside the Bash sandbox: run those node commands with dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true. Helpers: fun/transport/tests/browser-start.mjs exports createWorldFromMenu(page,{biome,size,seed,paused}), waitForGameReady, openGameAction. window.transport exposes {game, renderer, speed, setSpeed, setTool, setView, inspect, persist}. Use a fresh browser context (isolated localStorage). A working example tour script is at ${SP}/tour.mjs (menu → new world → panels → inspect → 8x speed, screenshots). Look at screenshots with the Read tool — actually look at the game, don't only read code.
For headless simulation experiments use node directly: import { createGame, tick, addRoute, ... } from the model (tests/helpers.mjs shows patterns such as emptyGame/advance).

HARD RULES: This is REVIEW ONLY. Do not edit, create or delete any file inside the repository (only inside your scratch dir). Do not commit. Do not start other servers on 8841.
CONSTRAINTS any proposal must respect: existing saves must keep loading (procedural world recipes 1-7 are digest-pinned; never change a released generation recipe); simulation determinism (randomAt-based seeded decisions, frame-rate independence); model.js must stay DOM-free; no build step or new runtime dependencies; the visual/UI design language already in place (warm off-white panels, dark green, orange accent, Space Grotesk, normal-case labels).

WHAT A GOOD PROPOSAL LOOKS LIKE: a concrete, player-visible improvement (or a measured perf win / real bug fix) with evidence — file:line references, a measurement, or a screenshot observation — plus an implementation sketch naming the functions/files to change, and a way to verify it. Prefer high-impact changes that fit the existing architecture. Aim for 6-10 strong proposals, ranked by impact; no vague ideas ("improve the UI"), no pure refactors without player/perf payoff.`

const PROPOSALS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    dimension: { type: 'string' },
    summary: { type: 'string', description: 'overall assessment of this dimension, 3-8 sentences, with the most important observations from actually playing' },
    proposals: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          id: { type: 'string', description: 'short kebab-case id, unique' },
          title: { type: 'string' },
          problem: { type: 'string', description: 'what is wrong or missing, with evidence (file:line, measurements, screenshot observations)' },
          proposal: { type: 'string', description: 'concrete design and implementation sketch: which functions/files change and how' },
          files: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
          impact: { type: 'integer', minimum: 1, maximum: 5 },
          effort: { type: 'string', enum: ['S', 'M', 'L'] },
          risk: { type: 'string', description: 'save compatibility, determinism, test breakage, design fit' },
          verification: { type: 'string', description: 'how to prove it works (tests to add/run, browser check)' },
        },
        required: ['id', 'title', 'problem', 'proposal', 'files', 'impact', 'effort', 'risk', 'verification'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['dimension', 'summary', 'proposals'],
}

const VERDICTS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    verdicts: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          id: { type: 'string' },
          verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['keep', 'revise', 'reject'] },
          reason: { type: 'string' },
          evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'what you checked: file:line, test run, browser observation' },
          revisedProposal: { type: 'string', description: 'for keep/revise: the corrected, precise implementation spec (functions, files, behavior, edge cases, save compat)' },
          impact: { type: 'integer', minimum: 1, maximum: 5 },
          effort: { type: 'string', enum: ['S', 'M', 'L'] },
        },
        required: ['id', 'verdict', 'reason', 'evidence', 'impact', 'effort'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['verdicts'],
}

const DIMENSIONS = [
  { key: 'fun-progression', focus: `FUN, CORE LOOP AND PROGRESSION. Is the game rewarding minute to minute and over an hour? Study the economy (model.js, industry-simulation.js, settlements.js, economy-pricing.js, data.js, gameplay-insights.js, the objective card in app.js). Run headless node simulations: how profitable is the starter bus, a typical first freight route (road vs rail vs ship), how long until the player can afford the next meaningful step, does money snowball or stall, are there dead stretches with nothing to do or decide? Are there goals/milestones beyond "First 100 deliveries", celebration moments, stakes (bankruptcy? loans?), reasons to build rail over road, reasons to grow towns? Propose concrete changes to make play more engaging: goal/milestone ladder, rewards, meaningful choices, balance fixes backed by numbers.` },
  { key: 'controls-construction', focus: `CONTROLS AND CONSTRUCTION EASE. Play it: build a road between a producer and its customer, place stops, create a freight route, all via real pointer events in Playwright; count clicks and friction points. Read the pointer/keyboard/tool code in app.js, construction-plan.js, terrain-engineering.js. Look for: how road/rail drag paths are chosen (straight lines vs auto-routing around obstacles/along valid slopes), whether a failed/slope-rejected drag explains itself and suggests a fix, undo, tool switching, hotkeys, snapping, preview clarity, cost preview, right-click/escape semantics, keyboard shortcut conflicts while typing in inputs, dragging on touch. Propose changes that make building faster and less frustrating.` },
  { key: 'routes-freedom', focus: `ROUTE MANAGEMENT AND PLAYER FREEDOM. The README scope says two-stop routes, one vehicle per route, no timetables. Study the Route/Vehicle model (model.js addRoute/removeRoute/vehicles/routeId, route-save-codec.js, fleet-save-codec.js) and the Routes panel UI in app.js/route-planner.js. Evaluate what would give players more freedom cheaply and safely: e.g. adding more vehicles to an existing route, editing a route's stops/cargo, duplicating/reversing routes, creating a route directly from a station or industry inspector ("Start route here"), naming, pausing a route, choosing an older/cheaper vehicle, feeder transfers, etc. For each, check model/save feasibility precisely (does the codec already allow multiple vehicles per routeId? what breaks?). Also play the route creation flow in the browser and identify friction.` },
  { key: 'ux-polish', focus: `UX SMOOTHNESS, FEEDBACK AND VISUAL POLISH. Play the game in the browser at 1440x900 desktop and look carefully at screenshots of every panel (Build tabs, Routes, Industries, Towns, Chains dialog, inspector for town/industry/station/vehicle, game menu, save dialog, layers, toasts, objective card, tooltips). Evaluate feedback moments: does a delivery show floating income over the vehicle? Is there sound (audio-button)? Do notifications/toasts communicate events well (new vehicle model, factory expansion, town growth, route broken)? Is there any animation/transition polish missing? Inconsistent spacing, clipped text, awkward states, empty states, confusing copy, all-caps eyebrows vs the normal-case style? Propose concrete, tasteful improvements that fit the existing design language.` },
  { key: 'onboarding', focus: `FIRST-SESSION EXPERIENCE. Play as a brand-new player from the main menu for the first ~10 minutes of game time in the browser. Is it clear what to do? Does "Your first cargo route" suggestion actually lead to a working route easily — can it highlight on the map, pan the camera there, pre-select tools, pre-fill the route form? Does the objective card appear and guide? Is the field guide useful? What is the time-to-first-profitable-freight-route and number of steps? Where would a new player get stuck (slopes rejecting roads, catchment confusion, which station covers which industry, route validation errors)? Propose a smoother onboarding (contextual hints, guided steps, one-click suggested connection, better error guidance) that fits the game.` },
  { key: 'map-readability', focus: `MAP READABILITY AND INFORMATION OVERLAYS. At Region/Town/Detail zoom in the browser, check: can you tell what each industry is and whether it is served? Station catchment visualization when placing a stop or hovering a station (5-tile radius, which industries/towns are covered)? Are vehicles visible enough (the starter bus looked tiny)? Route lines readable? Labels overlapping or cluttered? Do industry markers show supply/demand status (idle, needs input, full storage)? Can you see where cargo flows? Is there a visual cue for broken routes? Propose overlays/markers that make the map itself communicate game state, with renderer.js implementation sketches that respect the caching architecture (chunks, scenery batches, live overlays).` },
  { key: 'performance', focus: `PERFORMANCE. Measure, don't guess. In the browser (use a CDP session: Performance.getMetrics, Tracing or Profiler, or wrap functions) measure: startup-to-playable time and what dominates it (module loading of ~40 JS files, atlas decoding — the assets folder is 285 MB but only prefiltered atlases should load; verify what is actually fetched and its bytes), steady-state frame time at 1x and 8x speed on the default 512 map at Town and Region zoom, per-frame DOM work in app.js (panels/HUD/list rebuilds each frame or each tick? layout thrash? innerHTML churn?), simulation cost per tick at 8x with a grown network, GC churn, and memory. Also profile a larger 1024 world. Identify the top hot spots with numbers and propose optimizations that preserve exact behavior (the renderer has pixel-equivalence checks). Check PERFORMANCE.md for what was already optimized.` },
  { key: 'bugs-robustness', focus: `CORRECTNESS AND ROBUSTNESS THAT HURTS PLAYABILITY. Hunt real bugs in app.js/model.js/construction-plan.js/route-planner.js/settlements.js/save paths: keyboard shortcuts firing while typing in inputs or dialogs, Escape/right-click states that leave a tool stuck, money going negative with no recovery (can the player soft-lock with no income and no money?), routes silently broken, vehicles stuck, inspector showing stale data, toasts spamming, speed control not restored after dialogs, pointer capture leaks, window resize issues, errors in the console during play. Exercise edge cases in the browser (rapid tool switching, dragging off-canvas, building at map edges, bulldozing stations used by routes, loading while a tool is active) and in node. Every finding must be reproduced or pinned to exact code. Propose minimal robust fixes.` },
